Better automated option: Restic

Restic is designed for encrypted incremental backups. It backs up the real directory contents, including .gitignore files unless you explicitly exclude them.

Example:

$env:RESTIC_REPOSITORY = "D:\Backups\oppw-restic"
$env:RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE = "C:\Secure\restic-password.txt"

restic init
restic backup "D:\oppw"

Later backups only store changes:

restic backup "D:\oppw"

Verify the backup:

restic check
restic snapshots

Test restoring it:

restic restore latest --target "D:\RestoreTest"

Do not keep restic-password.txt only on the same disk as the backup. Losing that password makes the encrypted backup unrecoverable.

Another option: separate private secrets repository

You could create a second private repository containing configuration files, but it is less safe because:

Deleted secrets remain in Git history.
An account compromise exposes them.
API keys and passwords are easy to commit accidentally.

An encrypted backup is usually better for actual credentials.